<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego Velázquez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1599">1599</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Diego Rodríguez de Silva y Velázquez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego Velázquez was a Spanish painter, court painter to Philip IV and a leading artist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1623 and painted portraits of the royal family, historical scenes such as The Surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> and the celebrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-meninas">Las Meninas</a> of 1656. His naturalism and free brushwork influenced later painters from Goya to the Impressionists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez.jpg" alt="Diego Velázquez" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Velázquez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez_Autorretrato_45_x_38_cm_-_Colecci%C3%B3n_Real_Academia_de_Bellas_Artes_de_San_Carlos_-_Museo_de_Bellas_Artes_de_Valencia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
